WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.700
This is the first live cat recorded on film,
and it’s being dropped to solve a physics problem:

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.360
Why do cats always land on their
feet?

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.340
It’s a question that was driving 19th-century
scientists nuts.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.240
Until one of them used an unexpected tool
to solve the mystery: a camera.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:28.660
Étienne-Jules Marey was an obsessive scientist
and inventor who analyzed how things moved.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:32.750
And he started experimenting with photography
at a time when the medium was mostly used

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:34.579
to document static subjects.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.260
But his goal was to capture motion.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.739
And he did that by building on a basic principle
of photography: exposing a photosensitive

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:46.679
material to light and then covering it in
darkness.

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:53.260
So his way of creating this darkness and light
was to have a disc with slots in it.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.219
By controlling the light as the subject moved
across the frame, Marey was able to record

00:00:57.219 --> 00:00:59.090
movement onto a single glass plate.

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:04.049
Essentially, all he does is block that light
intermittently.

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:13.440
A slot from the disc opens, and then there’s
darkness as the man moves, opens, darkness.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.640
This technique is called chronophotography,
and the results show something human eyes

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.479
will never see on their own: individual stages
of motion.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.140
A couple of years later, Kodak introduced
celluloid film and Marey updated his slot

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.920
camera in a crucial way.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.649
He swapped the single glass plate with a roll
of film that moved in between exposures.

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:36.460
So, light: an image is made.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:37.990
Darkness, the film moves on.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:42.829
Light, an image — so it’s a movie camera,
is what it is.

00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:47.659
Marey made a lot of films for research purposes,
and even tried dropping other animals to see

00:01:47.659 --> 00:01:52.930
if they’d land on their feet, specifically
this rabbit and this chicken.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:54.800
Which brings us back to the cat.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:00.780
It seems to be able to right itself by flipping
in the air without pushing off anything first,

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.930
which would contradict the law of conservation
of angular momentum.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.880
Sounds scary, but stick with me here.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.000
One of Newton’s laws of motion says that
something in motion can’t just stop itself

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.320
unless an opposing force acts upon it.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.570
Basically, you can’t just change direction
midair, Wile E. Coyote style.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.530
But to the naked eye, it looks like a cat
can.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.250
Most people assumed the cat was “cheating”
by kicking off the hands of the person dropping

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.580
it, but Marey’s film showed what’s actually
happening.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.420
The first few frames prove right away that
the cat doesn’t start its rotation from

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.020
a kick.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.500
But what it does do is arch its back.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:38.500
And by arching its back, it’s divided its
body into a front part and a back part, and

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.760
the two parts can work independently.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.900
You know how a figure skater pulls their arms
in to rotate faster?

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.800
That’s what’s happening here too.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:50.220
Early in the rotation, the cat pulls its front
legs in and leaves the back splayed out so

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:54.730
the front half can rotate quickly while the
back half stays relatively still.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:56.860
Then halfway through, it does the opposite.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.600
Front legs stretched out, back ones tucked
in to flip the other half of its body around.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.740
And you notice by the time the cat is landing,
all four legs are stretched out as far as

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:09.400
they can be, which means slow rotation.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:16.410
So the cat has rotated itself, but not overall;
the two halves are working in opposite ways.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:20.490
It uses the inertia of its own bodyweight
to spin each side.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:25.340
And because the two spins operate separately
in opposing directions, they cancel each other out.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.160
So Newton’s law isn’t broken.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.620
Marey published his findings in Nature in
1894, breaking down the falling cat problem

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.160
for the first time.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.520
His work remains an early example of using
photography for scientific discovery.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:42.510
What does photography do for science?

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:47.980
It records something and it makes it permanent
so you can analyze it later, or so you can

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:48.680
share it.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.960
But what Marey did was show something that
the eye could not possibly see — ever.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:03.420
You might have seen another famous early example
of motion photography: In 1878, Eadweard Muybridge

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.720
used 12 cameras connected to tripwires to
prove that a horse lifts all four feet off

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.480
the ground at some point in a gallop.

